"Models the internal order of business activities, events and gateway choices within one participant.\n\nUse when: Explain how one organization handles a case. Specify exceptions, waits and parallel work precisely.\n\nAvoid when: The question concerns only exchanges among independent partners.\n\nVisual grammar: Rounded tasks perform work; circles denote events. Diamonds route tokens; sequence arrows remain within a pool. Boundary events attach to the task they interrupt or observe.\n\nBuild: Choose one participant and explicit start and completion events. Name tasks with verbs and label every conditional branch. Model waits, exceptions and joins before simplifying the main path.\n\nCheck: Sequence flows never cross pool boundaries. Gateway joins match intended concurrency, avoiding stranded tokens. Every reachable path completes, waits intentionally or terminates explicitly.\n\nPitfalls: Using message arrows as ordinary task order. Treating an exclusive choice as a parallel split.\n\nExample: An expense claim starts on submission, branches for approval, waits for correction when rejected and ends after reimbursement.\n\nAlternatives: BPMN collaboration, Swimlane flowchart."
